      ],
      "reversed": "Reversed letters: the eight smallest letters Dee picked out, which spell Paraoan, the name of a governor; Casaubon's print marks three of them reversed, P and A in its margin and the averted L Dee asked about in its type.",
      "sigils": "Sigils: none. The Golden Dawn's are taken from the Sigillum Dei Aemeth; the pages of Dee's record cited here draw none above the tables.",
      "differs": {
        "gd": "It is the table before the reform of 1587 that the Golden Dawn's higher letters mostly follow, so it keeps the older letters there, Earth's eighth row unshifted. It has two empty squares in Fire, Casaubon's capitals, and the eight reversed letters Dee picked out, the same eight the Golden Dawn reverses. It has no Tablet of Union, which is the Golden Dawn's.",
        "regardie": "Where Regardie prints two letters, his lower is often this table's and his higher the 1587 one. It also lacks the letters of Regardie's that are in neither of Dee's tables, and two of its Fire squares are empty.",
        "equinox": "The Equinox mostly follows this table where Dee's two differ, so the two are close: they differ in a few look-alike letters, in Earth's eighth row, in case, and in the two Fire squares this table leaves empty.",
        "raphael": "Raphael's table of 1587 is this one reformed: Earth's eighth row moved one square left, the two empty Fire squares filled and a third changed, and a few letters changed elsewhere. The 1659 print sets it in small letters, so the case differs too."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "raphael",
      "title": "Raphael's, 1587",
      "text": "The Tabula recensa, the table \"reformed by Raphael\" on 20 April 1587, as Casaubon prints it from Dee's record. Dee notes that he had been unsure in 1584 how to join the four tables, given apart, and the reformed table joins them. Its letters are as printed, where every other copy of Dee's tables agrees with the print; in ten squares the print has a letter no other copy of Dee's tables has, and this version corrects it, keeping the printed letter. It is set in small letters: the capitals of Dee's copy in Sloane MS 3191 are known here only through modern redrawings, and wait until the British Library's images return. In two Fire squares the print has x and Sloane MS 3191 p; this version follows the print, with p as a second letter.",
      "sources": [
