        },
        {
          "work": "casaubon1659",
          "part": "Actio Tertia",
          "page": 15,
          "leaf": 456
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "doubled",
      "title": "Squares with two letters",
      "text": "Regardie prints a second, smaller letter in many squares, and once four. He treats either letter as valid for the square, prefers the higher, and allows both, and the Order's names sometimes draw on both; Dee too was told that two readings of a name could both be true. Set against Dee's two tables, many of Regardie's pairs are the 1584 letter and the 1587 one, nearly always with the 1587 letter on top, and Earth's eighth row is the 1587 row over the 1584 row. So the Golden Dawn's tablets look like Dee's 1584 table with the 1587 readings written in. That is an inference from the letters: no Golden Dawn paper read here says so. Most of Regardie's other pairs are look-alike letters, whose source is open.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "work": "regardie1940",
          "page": 306,
          "leaf": 298
        },
        {
          "work": "regardie1940",
          "page": 272,
          "leaf": 264
        },
        {
          "work": "casaubon1659",
          "page": 188,
          "leaf": 277
        },
        {
          "work": "casaubon1659",
          "part": "Actio Tertia",
          "page": 15,
          "leaf": 456
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "reversed",
      "title": "Reversed letters",
      "text": "Dee picked out eight letters smaller than the rest, one or two in each table, which together spell Paraoan, the name of a governor; the eighth, an L, he was told could be read N or L. Casaubon's print marks some of them reversed. The Equinox writes the same eight in Enochian, since a reversed A or O cannot be told from a plain one; Greer mirrors N, L, R and P, the four whose reversal can be seen; Regardie prints them plain.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "work": "casaubon1659",
