      },
      {
        "row": 7,
        "column": 0,
        "note": "Row 8 is where the versions differ most. The reform of 1587 moved Dee's 1584 row one square to the left, dropping this square's reversed R and adding t at the end. Regardie prints the two rows together, the 1587 one above, except in this square, where the 1584 R stands over the 1587 o; the Golden Dawn's version reads his higher letters. The Equinox and Greer begin the row with the reversed R, and Casaubon prints it as a ligature. That Regardie's two rows are Dee's two tables is an inference from the letters.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "work": "regardie1940",
            "page": 272,
            "leaf": 264
          },
          {
            "work": "equinox1912",
            "plate": "III",
            "leaf": 241
          },
          {
            "work": "greer"
          },
          {
            "work": "casaubon1659",
            "page": 176,
            "leaf": 265
          },
          {
            "work": "casaubon1659",
            "part": "Actio Tertia",
            "page": 15,
            "leaf": 456
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "row": 7,
        "column": 7,
        "note": "Regardie prints i and a side by side over b and t; the Golden Dawn's version takes i, which the Equinox, Greer and the 1584 table have; the 1587 table has a.",
        "sources": [
          {
            "work": "regardie1940",
            "page": 272,
            "leaf": 264
          },
          {
            "work": "equinox1912",
            "plate": "III",
